#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "2d9fc36f-8d8f-5e57-9ff0-28a032e71980" :question/math-academy-id "e-3726" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/0811362e235ec7c6e93a8bf892a451f8c347d0f633f251b9ed955062a53dd6c3.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where $f'(x) = 0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, $f'(2)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since it is the highest point of a peak in a graph.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point at $x=2$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "97897d09-3dc9-5e35-8f56-d44f54c542cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6169" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/50581635273c185b9ca3aefa907b2e2a4981ec1bf6be1c687bec47591e126a89.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?" :question/worked-solution "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where $f'(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.  \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have $f'(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/56e798c0942ddcf0b1786afb054ab6dfbea7768c86751555808d86cc4cfa89f6.png)\n\n  \n\nSo, there are two stationary points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=0$ and $x=4$.\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so $f'(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19509a20-4416-55b8-985a-d2da1754bcc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280575} {:answer/id #uuid "56caf002-ef8f-5f09-a5b4-d1138de10f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the closed interval $[-2,2]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280576} {:answer/id #uuid "5093fe11-129e-5e5a-918a-c8885801b1ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the open interval $(-2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280577} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3de4b7-7d09-56a4-80a5-4dd4b5b6e3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the closed intervals $(-5,-2]$ and $[2,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280578} {:answer/id #uuid "cb112ede-bd16-5541-9627-5700d8f98df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the intervals $(-5,-2)$ and $(2,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb112ede-bd16-5541-9627-5700d8f98df2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the intervals $(-5,-2)$ and $(2,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280579} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5aa1f45-10ca-521e-9aff-a2310eeeead9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "399a7648-b41d-51df-9f7e-45ef9ac8e709" :question/math-academy-id "q-103432" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/55d089ab7c7947e3f9d67a11fd69fa3903abd6f10ef09853fe018eaa16524ec1.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the stationary points?" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between $x=-5$ and $x=-2$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at those points,\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=5$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at those points,\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/6983f3615bf721529173c11de3a9218962a57e4be5247f4bda1ac2fa496531ee.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$ are all values in the intervals $(-5,-2)$ and $(2,5).$ \n\n**Note:** At $x=-2$ and $x=2$, there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points, but they are not stationary points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "692bcfab-a633-5b18-9ce0-ddabf9fc5e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in {-1,0}\\cup [2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280582} {:answer/id #uuid "820a4ea4-e49a-52db-872f-67598e3abb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in {-3,-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280583} {:answer/id #uuid "161b45b3-450d-5498-b630-360399b890ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in {-3,-1}\\cup (2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280584} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f89571-144c-55ea-bf8a-4e07b6f1cc38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280585} {:answer/id #uuid "e03e9ab1-4602-5166-aa1e-5d30756fe5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in {-3,-1,0,2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "161b45b3-450d-5498-b630-360399b890ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in {-3,-1}\\cup (2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280584} :answer-field/id #uuid "5124d7b7-38e7-515e-a83e-0f09948badb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d36563a-6e2c-5e9e-9cc2-a7be86e291cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-103434" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/07c617933910b3d82ed397fd77754beb2b49fef9bfb498dea3f51a392f6c1d03.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the stationary points?" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at $x=-3$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(-3)=0,$\n\n- at $x=-1$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(-1)=0,$\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=4$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/d8a2a08999b45a256fc0458069e50771c2c4ee03ab4f2ed1166f3aa8a1c70c83.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=-1,$ and all values in the open interval $(2,4).$\n\n**Note:** At $x=0$ and $x=2$ there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points, but they are not stationary points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f3a6672-cc58-591c-a3bf-893dfc77f294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280589} {:answer/id #uuid "2cce352f-dc8a-5166-b606-bf2ad69140bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280590} {:answer/id #uuid "daafd825-db15-5209-817d-4a5efa1ed438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280591} {:answer/id #uuid "b8605e9e-e8d0-50ff-9d5a-9d56e6f5753d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280592} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2cff82-160c-55aa-8462-3e6c2f821b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cce352f-dc8a-5166-b606-bf2ad69140bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280590} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2bbcaf3-e66e-5528-969d-a32a23b50308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc25f65f-6157-58dc-8973-bfb8847f3624" :question/math-academy-id "q-103442" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/7dc7480c7ee27073ff7be7475161742e317b30bf0c3e35af33dc93a772e6034a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=-1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=3$ since $f(3)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, while statements II and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90a80798-1acb-5c8c-9c73-003aeeca8168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280596} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f7dc24-7f82-5eae-ab2f-0810d3845890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280597} {:answer/id #uuid "709e56e6-2610-5af0-a991-a0856ce8ac5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280598} {:answer/id #uuid "8d31cb47-0e46-5186-8664-e1c5c23d3e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280599} {:answer/id #uuid "3c563599-2a2f-54e7-a9ec-1f2eda3b11b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7f7dc24-7f82-5eae-ab2f-0810d3845890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280597} :answer-field/id #uuid "771a0a25-0693-5931-a5ce-28de845dbde3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62b39803-cf8b-5f2a-91f9-b83f48f12d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47919" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/d9d8fb6d663d8dfd0cce24ae3817c936e2e97ab2df7aefeaa0c734caf0311b94.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-3,10]$, which points represent the global maximum of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The global extrema of a function are the lowest or highest points of the function's graph over its entire domain.\n\nThe lowest point is the global minimum, whereas the highest point is the global maximum.\n\nAccording to the graph, the highest points are $B$ and $D$, and so these points represents the global maximum of $f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a911a354-05f2-54ed-853b-27683fe56471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063018} {:answer/id #uuid "b25ccfec-f7c3-56a1-a041-2552a060867e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063019} {:answer/id #uuid "d8eeb976-fc52-55bd-a1e7-3cc8b5b0d50f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063020} {:answer/id #uuid "1796b9dc-7c2c-5e7e-8db0-0abc6981873f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063021} {:answer/id #uuid "2161585b-4842-5215-b26a-c9eda7d2171c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b25ccfec-f7c3-56a1-a041-2552a060867e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063019} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77652f4-7c3d-5eee-8f7e-57169052f789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9157fc37-ed6c-586e-91c2-30b330ac595f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48689" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/bb17384f2313820f526f1ba4356f6f3c9e11fbb3791e4f7e2c6044cc604bf662.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of $f(x)$ has a has a sharp edge (cusp) at $x=2.$  Therefore, ${f}^{′}(2)$ does not exist and hence it can't be equal to zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since $f(x)\\le f(2)$ for $x$ near the point $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=2$ since the derivative does not exist at this point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba56ec51-0bdd-5152-8945-39ae8bedd052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280603} {:answer/id #uuid "ef95798c-ff6e-534f-935a-e47b1e23d900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280604} {:answer/id #uuid "65897179-1624-586f-83d7-cb1f04490553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280605} {:answer/id #uuid "3d5d1f4b-9b21-5ee4-8851-623ea3a7adda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280606} {:answer/id #uuid "f83b5b45-6a2a-5f8c-9b5b-00c3448fa764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65897179-1624-586f-83d7-cb1f04490553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280605} :answer-field/id #uuid "46661a1d-3864-5a96-a5e2-b5253f52d20d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dce9cb91-9336-5a8f-9d65-459010bc2821" :question/math-academy-id "q-48734" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/14fdc374842064ad40ebbadf3dab16191ecfdeec8c416b4e7f17beffce44fb40.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=3$\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=3$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=3.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(3)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement II is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the derivative is undefined at $x=3$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function does not have a local maximum at $x=3$ since $f(3)\\le f(x)$ for $x$ close enough  to the point $x=3,$ so,  actually,  $f(x)$ has local minimum at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bfacec1-1bdf-5550-9d6c-13eb465aaf8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280610} {:answer/id #uuid "c0220f13-86a9-5209-ba9a-13e1418a7b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280611} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed100cd-2886-5b4c-8a2d-5cfbe8c75069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280612} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab56549-2e7c-5847-83cf-24e69d803d84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280613} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c2b907-1524-5970-b8e8-e077011d69ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ab56549-2e7c-5847-83cf-24e69d803d84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280613} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b13adb5-9271-58df-a814-55e4bc278f28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1648c41-f8a3-54c4-a6c4-08a83701a800" :question/math-academy-id "q-48737" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/4a2298a8255a86c803560793658593a22f7f77590ef350c1e0cebd974fce7750.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has  a local maximum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=0$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since $f(2)\\ge f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a sharp edge (corner) at $x=2$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(2)$ does not exist. Hence, $x=2$ is a critical point of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, all statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e01eed10-4f2d-50b4-ae2f-8468ea94f0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280617} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b16167-95ec-5d45-a09a-860730a4ddff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$  and $x=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280618} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e0e7e0-d0d2-5d00-be37-746b1796e279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280619} {:answer/id #uuid "484d8f9b-d06a-5592-a30b-47258a4acfdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280620} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd1b7ee-b130-5178-809d-86ca37411752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "484d8f9b-d06a-5592-a30b-47258a4acfdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280620} :answer-field/id #uuid "db7b9a07-56fe-5751-bb1b-51c088e24cdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ece606bc-4b2a-5813-a3c1-ba2ac8790c10" :question/math-academy-id "q-73399" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/432991e403167bd560517072d24058c3f2106f093cdcad08f3b5f0ed486f9504.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively. \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that \n\n- at $x=3$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(3)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=7$ there is a discontinuity, so  ${f}^{′}(7)$ doesn't exist.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/e035d64e759108be78c54d0d8d1018906ef253bdcdaff548edd185b8b6543505.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two critical points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=3$ and $x=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c677eed-072e-5a9d-ba1e-38ed2a01a256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280624} {:answer/id #uuid "cd25040a-e948-5636-bdbe-1cc158adf2f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280625} {:answer/id #uuid "6b655f12-b071-5d9e-89b0-51c0d15f6c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280626} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fb4479-a57e-50a2-8f0e-db3cffbe2cc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280627} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ed7f70-685a-5c90-960a-8487eb65e6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the closed interval $[2,6]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1ed7f70-685a-5c90-960a-8487eb65e6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the closed interval $[2,6]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280628} :answer-field/id #uuid "09e609df-9895-5b71-b35b-1a65a8539dcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fc62285-d6ad-563b-a202-c3fe4f3d9e71" :question/math-academy-id "q-73409" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/7c5bc75101a3bf4b4074bf60c1ab68962f2ed0de559f889365c3807be2fa833c.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the critical points?" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that\n\n- at any point between $x=2$ and $x=6$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=2$ and at $x=6$ there are sharp corners, hence ${f}^{′}(2)$ and ${f}^{′}(6)$ don't exist.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/6e4299d762e26ff3de85ae0fd766d28368de937098dfcf232ce9a234fee826e1.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, all numbers in the closed interval $[2,6]$ are critical points for $f(x).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b7d219a-7641-5c12-bde8-d0293a6991df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280631} {:answer/id #uuid "19ef078c-fc7b-5dc6-b320-c7b3822c1831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$, $x=4$, and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280632} {:answer/id #uuid "b5cc5877-0fd0-55f0-b3d5-948650662c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the open interval $(3,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280633} {:answer/id #uuid "3db9a6ff-5921-53c5-8477-0d996f15af4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280634} {:answer/id #uuid "afb79966-ed68-5f2c-a1bb-8d8a256df246" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5cc5877-0fd0-55f0-b3d5-948650662c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All points in the open interval $(3,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280633} :answer-field/id #uuid "57b8d7f0-90ca-560f-832e-286b4995ff94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a831b1fe-cda9-5ab9-bf01-7acc19edee37" :question/math-academy-id "q-73410" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/eab84ed04b86678fb4a8d23c010e9a22beead062595b49818ac95e996c3173e6.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above. What are the $x$-coordinates of all the stationary points?" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at any point between $x=3$ and $x=4$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at those points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980112/assets/ab200bebf80bbd1abe6e0c43ec124fe359da25f38d4346d25d8741decc88aa26.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points of $f(x)$, are all values in the open interval $(3,4).$ \n\n**Note:** At $x=3$ and $x=4$, there are sharp corners, so ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist at those points. This gives us two more critical points. But they are not stationary points."}]}
